WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.994
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:10.742
[CHALK SCRATCH]

00:00:10.742 --> 00:00:13.200
PETER GARFIELD: This video is
meant as a quick introduction

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.477
to phase plane analysis
and the use of nullclines.

00:00:16.477 --> 00:00:19.560
We're going to get a sense of what
solutions to the system of differential

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:25.110
equations-- dx dt equals
0.2x minus 0.1 x y,

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:30.630
and dy dt equals minus
0.15y plus 0.05 xy--

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.130
look like.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:36.030
This system is meant to be analogous
to the systems of sardines and marlins

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.300
in the other videos.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.350
But more mathematically familiar.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:44.310
We'll be using x and y rather than
s for sardines and m for marlins.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.070
Let's start with what we can
say about the solution curve

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.140
through a particular point.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.740
As an example, let's pick the
point x, y equals four, one.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:58.290
What can we say about a solution
curve passing through this point?

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.910
How is x changing?

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:08.370
The differential equation for x tells
us that dx dt equals 0.2x minus 0.1 x y,

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:14.690
which is 0.2 times four minus 0.1
times four times one, which is 0.4.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:18.300
It's not so critical at this point that
we know the value of this derivative.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.520
But it's nice to know the sign.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:26.980
The fact that it is positive tells
us that x is increasing with time.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:35.250
Similarly, dy dt equals
negative 0.15y plus 0.05 xy,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:41.050
which is negative 0.15 one
plus 0.05 times four times one.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.040
This tells us y is increasing with time.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.380
This means that any solution curve
passing through the point four,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.510
one has x increasing with time.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.220
The curve is moving to the right.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.050
It has y increasing with time.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.820
The curve is moving up.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.120
We represent this with
an arrow on our graph.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.390
Now we can continue in this
way taking random points

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.220
to get a feel for our solution curves.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:10.199
But we're going to try to be a little
more thoughtful and systematic.

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:13.380
Where will our solution
curves move to the right?

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.650
That is, where is dx dt positive?

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.050
And where will the
curves move to the left?

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.600
Where is dx dt negative?

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:27.150
The easiest way to approach this is
to first find where dx dt is zero.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:35.760
Let's factor dx dt equals 0.2x minus 0.1
times x times y into 0.1 times x times

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.550
the quantity two minus y.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:45.780
We see that dx dt is zero when x
equals zero or when y equals two.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.200
Let's draw these lines in.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.560
These lines are where dx dt is zero.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.110
So we've also drawn in
vertical line segments

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:59.370
to indicate that the solution curves
pass over these lines vertically.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:03.540
Because x is not changing,
there's no movement right or left.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.440
These lines are called nullclines,
where the null indicates

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.100
that the derivative is zero.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:15.300
Now where are our solution
curves moving to the right?

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.680
Where x is increasing or
where dx dt is positive.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.050
Similarly, our solution
curves are moving to the left

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.550
where x is decreasing.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.430
That is, where dx dt is negative.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.900
These regions are actually
determined by where dx dt is zero.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.870
We can tell which region
has positive derivative

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:38.190
and which has negative derivative
by simply testing a sample point.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:42.180
We've already seen that dx dt is
positive at the point four, one.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.960
And it is a simple computation
to see that dx dt is negative

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.420
when x y equals one, three.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:53.860
dx dt is 0.2 times x
minus 0.1 times x times y,

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:56.580
which is 0.2 times one
minus 0.1 times one times

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:01.390
three, which is 0.2 minus
0.3, which is negative 0.1.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:08.280
We can do a very similar computation
with dy dt equals negative 0.15y plus

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:15.855
0.05 x y, which equals 0.05 y times the
quantity minus three plus x to get dy

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:21.240
dt zero on the lines y equals
zero and x equals three.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.620
As before, the nullclines represent
where the derivative is zero.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.160
In this case, the derivative is dy dt.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.710
So where this is zero is where
the solution curves are only

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:35.010
moving horizontally, not vertically.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:39.450
Thus they have small horizontal
line segments to represent this.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.630
And again, we've tested points to
determine which region the solution

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:43.870
curves go up--

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.330
that is, where dy dt is positive--

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.760
and in which region the
solution curves go down--

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.510
that is, where dy dt is negative.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:56.370
We finally put this all together
to get a picture like this.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.850
Now we're going to redraw this
and simplify it in a few ways.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:04.260
Every pair of arrows is going to
be replaced by a diagonal arrow.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.870
Also the short lines
across the nullclines

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:11.460
are extended to be arrows to show which
direction the solution curves travel.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.600
Finally, points where
both dx dt and dy dt

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:19.290
are zero, where nullclines from the
two equations cross, are shown as dots.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.000
These are equilibrium points.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.530
This gives us an idea of what our
solution curves might look like.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.050
It looks like they
travel counter-clockwise

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:32.160
around our equilibrium point
at the point three, two.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.525
They might be elliptical or circular.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:43.260
Or perhaps they spiral
outward or even spiral inward.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:50.880
This phase plane analysis is an
example of qualitative analysis.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.660
While in general it's difficult to
solve a system of equations like this,

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.270
the phase plane analysis
allows us to understand

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.180
some of the general
features of solution curves.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

